IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @design

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
# Design purpose

Design records allocate requirements to architecture, components, interfaces,
and consequential decisions.

Use immutable UIDs with this form:

```text
DES-[MODULE]-[TYPE]-[NNN]
```

Supported type segments are `ARCH`, `CMP`, `IF`, and `DEC`.

Design depth is not a safety classification:

- `Basic` establishes boundaries, responsibilities, and direct allocation
- `Standard` also establishes interfaces, dependencies, and decisions
- `Deep` also establishes detailed contracts, failure behavior, safety
  boundaries, interference controls, degraded behavior, and verification
  implications.

Safety-critical components and safety-boundary interfaces normally require
`Deep` design.

Classify a design as:

- `NotSafetyRelated` only when it cannot reasonably affect a hazard, safety
  function, risk control, or safety assumption
- `SafetyRelated` when it supports or can interfere with safety behavior
- `SafetyCritical` when it implements or allocates a safety requirement,
  function, integrity target, or required fault response.
<<<

[DESIGN]
UID: DES-TEMPLATE-ARCH-001
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Template system architecture
TYPE: DESIGN
DESIGN_TYPE: Architecture
DESIGN_DEPTH: Deep
SAFETY_RELEVANCE: SafetyRelated
STATEMENT: The system is organized into explicitly owned components, with safety-critical responsibilities contained within a defined safety boundary and lower-assurance interactions constrained by controlled interfaces.
RATIONALE: The architecture separates safety responsibilities from non-safety services while retaining explicit integration and shared-resource obligations.
SAFETY_BOUNDARY: >>>
Boundary name: Template safety boundary
Components inside: Template safety controller
Components outside: Template communications component and external services
Protected safety requirements: REQ-TEMPLATE-SAFE-001
Protected safety functions: SAFE-TEMPLATE-FUNC-001
Permitted interactions: Versioned, validated, bounded requests and status
Prohibited interactions: Unrestricted safety commands and direct modification of protected parameters
External assumptions: Replace with reviewed assumptions
Failure-containment objective: Lower-assurance failures do not prevent the required safe response
Evidence required: Interface, robustness, resource, timing, and fault-injection evidence
<<<
INTERFERENCE_CONTROLS: >>>
Command validation: Validate identity, authorization, state, range, and sequence
Data validation: Validate schema, range, freshness, sequence, and integrity
Timeout and fallback: Enter the defined fallback state within a bounded time
Resource partitioning: Bound CPU, memory, storage, network, and logging consumption
Configuration authority: Restrict changes to protected configuration
Monitoring: Detect communication, resource, and dependency failures
Common-cause controls: Identify shared power, timing, tools, and update mechanisms
<<<
CONSTRAINTS: Replace with platform, deployment, timing, power, environmental, security, and compliance constraints.
CONSEQUENCES: Changes to component allocation, boundaries, shared resources, or interface semantics require safety-impact analysis and integration regression.
TAGS: template, architecture, safety-boundary
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: REQ-TEMPLATE-FR-001
  ROLE: Implements or refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: REQ-TEMPLATE-SAFE-001
  ROLE: Implements or refines

[DESIGN]
UID: DES-TEMPLATE-CMP-001
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Template non-safety-related component
TYPE: DESIGN
DESIGN_TYPE: Component
DESIGN_DEPTH: Standard
SAFETY_RELEVANCE: NotSafetyRelated
STATEMENT: The communications component provides operational communication and storage without implementing a safety function.
RATIONALE: Its responsibilities are operational, and safety-relevant interactions are constrained by a separate safety-boundary interface.
SAFETY_BOUNDARY: The component remains outside the template safety boundary.
INTERFERENCE_CONTROLS: >>>
Permitted interactions: Bounded requests and status
Prohibited interactions: Direct actuator control and protected parameter modification
Input and command validation: Performed by the receiving safety-related component
Timeout and fallback: The receiving component does not depend on timely communication for safe response
Resource limits: CPU, memory, storage, bandwidth, and logging limits are enforced
Configuration and updates: Versioned and authorized
Monitoring: Health and resource status are observable
<<<
CONSTRAINTS: The component must comply with the approved interface revision and resource limits.
CONSEQUENCES: Its lightweight verification does not replace receiving-system integration and containment evidence.
TAGS: template, component, non-safety
